AbLafeqrnGQ/h17/h001.apeP06H-moCRSZA-ohi5zMk5ZWuJnFUKm23i6Nq1Pwg" style=3D"=
color: #0077B6;">=E2=80=98Dystopian=E2=80=99: Co-op becomes latest firm to =
put staff under AI surveillance</a></strong></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Co-op said it did not recognize the cr=
iticism and said its probate advisers were highly engaged.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Resentment of workers=E2=80=99 treatment i=
s already translating into policy, a break from the sluggishness of most te=
chnology regulations. Across the Atlantic and the continental United States=
, California governor Gavin Newsom signed laws into effect last week that p=
rohibit certain types of workplace surveillance.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">California=E2=80=99s laws aim to target wo=
rkplace surveillance measures like heat maps that track employees=E2=80=99 =
movements, including how long they spend in the bathroom, or having their e=
motional states monitored. Amazon warehouse workers have previously complai=
ned about being timed on their bathroom breaks, for example. And at Kaiser =
Permanente, a California hospital chain, nurses have said automated systems=
 rated their tone of voice in patient interactions.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Read more: <a href=3D"https://abli=
nk.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszc=
